  <!-- JSON-LD: FAQPage -->
  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the Per Drop More Crop (PDMC) scheme and how do I apply?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "PDMC is a component of PMKSY that provides financial assistance for micro-irrigation. Small/marginal farmers get 55% subsidy, others get 45%. To apply, visit your state's agriculture department portal or Common Service Centre (CSC) with land records and Aadhaar. The subsidy is now transferred directly (DBT) to reduce delays."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why is micro-irrigation adoption still low among small farmers if it's so beneficial?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "While the technology is proven, the adoption gap is real. Key barriers include high upfront capital costs, delays in subsidy disbursement (reported in ~46% of cases in some regions), and a lack of technical knowledge for maintenance. The government is addressing this via the Micro Irrigation Fund and digital DBT, but awareness and last-mile credit access remain challenges."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the groundwater situation in India's rice-wheat belt?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "It is critical. In Haryana, the stage of groundwater extraction is 136.75%, with some districts exceeding 200%. In Punjab, water tables are falling by 0.4–1.0 meters per year, and 111–115 of 153 assessment units are over-exploited. Solutions require a landscape-scale approach—not just farm-level efficiency, but changing cropping patterns and incentivizing low-water crops."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What are the most cost-effective water-saving techniques for a smallholder?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Laser land leveling (ensures uniform water distribution), mulching (reduces evaporation), and Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) are low-cost, high-impact agronomic practices. When combined with a subsidized drip system for high-value crops, the payback period can be 0.5 to 2 years."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How does community participation help in water management?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The Hiware Bazar model in Maharashtra is the gold standard. Through integrated watershed development and Gram Sabha participation, the village raised its water table from 20–25 feet to 70–80 feet, changed cropping patterns to high-value horticulture, and saw reverse migration. It proves that top-down policy must be paired with local ownership for long-term sustainability."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

