    <!-- ============ TL;DR (ANSWER-FIRST) ============ -->
    <section class="tldr-section">
      <div class="container">
        <article class="tldr-box">
          <h2>What is Agricultural Water Management in India?</h2>
          <p>Agricultural water management in India is the strategic practice of optimizing water use to ensure food security amid severe groundwater depletion. The core solution is transitioning from flood irrigation to <strong>micro-irrigation (drip/sprinkler)</strong>, which achieves <strong>80–95% efficiency</strong>, supported by <strong>PMKSY subsidies</strong> (55% for small farmers) and practices like <strong>Direct Seeded Rice</strong> and <strong>laser land leveling</strong>.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ THREE PILLARS ============ -->
    <section class="pillars" id="technologies">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>The Three Pillars of India’s Water Management Strategy</h2>
        <div class="pillar-grid">
          <article class="pillar-card">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">💧</div>
            <h3>Technology &amp; On-Farm Efficiency (The “Hardware”)</h3>
            <p><strong>Micro-Irrigation (Drip &amp; Sprinkler):</strong> Delivers water directly to the root zone, minimizing evaporation and runoff. Drip systems are the gold standard for water-scarce regions, achieving up to 90–95% efficiency.</p>
            <p><strong>Precision Scheduling:</strong> Moving beyond fixed timers. Using <strong>soil moisture sensors</strong> and <strong>evapotranspiration (ET) models</strong> to irrigate only when the crop needs it, saving up to <strong>30–45%</strong> water compared to traditional methods.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="pillar-card">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">🏛️</div>
            <h3>Policy &amp; Economic Incentives (The “Enabler”)</h3>
            <p><strong>PMKSY &amp; PDMC:</strong> The umbrella scheme (“Har Khet Ko Pani”) provides the financial backbone. The <strong>“Per Drop More Crop” (PDMC)</strong> component offers <strong>55% subsidy</strong> for small/marginal farmers and <strong>45%</strong> for others to install micro-irrigation systems.</p>
            <p><strong>Micro Irrigation Fund (MIF):</strong> Managed by <a href="https://www.nabard.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NABARD</a>, this provides additional state-level top-up subsidies, making the upfront capital cost less prohibitive for smallholders.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="pillar-card">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">🌱</div>
            <h3>Agronomy &amp; Landscape Practices (The “Method”)</h3>
            <p><strong>Crop Diversification:</strong> Shifting away from water-intensive paddy in the Northwest (Punjab/Haryana) towards pulses, oilseeds, and millets. This is critical as Haryana’s groundwater extraction is at <strong>136.75%</strong>.</p>
            <p><strong>Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) &amp; AWD:</strong> DSR eliminates the need for continuous flooding during transplantation. <strong>Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD)</strong> can reduce water use by up to 30% without yield loss.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

