        </div>

        <div class="apply-grid">
          <div class="apply-box">
            <h3>Required Documents Checklist</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Aadhaar Card</strong> linked with an active bank account (for DBT).</li>
              <li><strong>Land Ownership Record</strong> (7/12 extract, Khatauni, Jamabandi, or RoR).</li>
              <li><strong>Bank Passbook Copy</strong> showing IFSC code and account number.</li>
              <li><strong>Caste Certificate</strong> (for SC/ST additional state grants, where applicable).</li>
              <li><strong>Quotation / Invoice</strong> from a state-empanelled BIS-certified micro-irrigation vendor.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="apply-box">
            <h3>How to Apply Online (Official Portals)</h3>
            <ol>
              <li>Register on your state agriculture DBT portal (for example, <a href="https://mahadbt.maharashtra.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MahaDBT</a> in Maharashtra) or the national <a href="https://pdmc.da.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PDMC Portal</a> / <a href="https://pmksy.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PMKSY Portal</a>.</li>
              <li>Select <em>Horticulture / Micro-Irrigation (Per Drop More Crop)</em> and submit crop and survey details.</li>
              <li>Receive pre-sanction approval and install the BIS-certified system through a registered vendor.</li>
              <li>Complete geo-tagged field verification by the agriculture officer for DBT subsidy release.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ FAQ (NATIVE HTML5 DETAILS/SUMMARY) ============ -->
    <section class="faq" id="faq">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2>
        <div class="accordion">

          <details open>
            <summary>What is the “Per Drop More Crop” (PDMC) scheme and how do I apply?</summary>
            <div class="accordion-body">
              <p>PDMC is a component of PMKSY that provides financial assistance for micro-irrigation. Small/marginal farmers get <strong>55% subsidy</strong>, others get <strong>45%</strong>. To apply, visit your state’s agriculture department portal or Common Service Centre (CSC) with land records and Aadhaar. The subsidy is now transferred directly (DBT) to reduce delays.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Why is micro-irrigation adoption still low among small farmers if it’s so beneficial?</summary>
            <div class="accordion-body">
              <p>While the technology is proven, the <strong>adoption gap</strong> is real. Key barriers include <strong>high upfront capital costs</strong>, <strong>delays in subsidy disbursement</strong> (reported in ~46% of cases in some regions), and a lack of technical knowledge for maintenance. The government is addressing this via the Micro Irrigation Fund and digital DBT, but awareness and last-mile credit access remain challenges.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What is the groundwater situation in India’s rice-wheat belt?</summary>
            <div class="accordion-body">
              <p>It is <strong>critical</strong>. In Haryana, the stage of groundwater extraction is <strong>136.75%</strong>, with some districts exceeding 200%. In Punjab, water tables are falling by <strong>0.4–1.0 meters per year</strong>, and 111–115 of 153 assessment units are “over-exploited”. Solutions require a “landscape-scale” approach—not just farm-level efficiency, but changing cropping patterns and incentivizing low-water crops.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What are the most cost-effective water-saving techniques for a smallholder?</summary>
            <div class="accordion-body">
              <p><strong>Laser land leveling</strong> (ensures uniform water distribution), <strong>mulching</strong> (reduces evaporation), and <strong>Direct Seeded Rice (DSR)</strong> are low-cost, high-impact agronomic practices. When combined with a subsidized drip system for high-value crops, the payback period can be <strong>0.5 to 2 years</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>How does community participation help in water management?</summary>
            <div class="accordion-body">
              <p>The <strong>Hiware Bazar model</strong> in Maharashtra is the gold standard. Through integrated watershed development and <em>Gram Sabha</em> participation, the village raised its water table from 20–25 feet to 70–80 feet, changed cropping patterns to high-value horticulture, and saw <strong>reverse migration</strong>. It proves that top-down policy must be paired with local ownership for long-term sustainability.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

